  "live_records": {
    "q-147235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q147235",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
