    "q-148666": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148666",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45%$ of the entire production, and $5%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
